  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1142 AM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nREMNANTS OF RACHEL BRINGS GUSTY WINDS, STRONGER STORMS: \n\nRachel made its transition from a tropical cyclone to an extra-\ntropical system near Las Vegas this morning. As Rachel's \nremnants continue to advect north/northeastward over the next \n24 hours, increasing wind gusts and wet weather will drive the \nforecast. Impacts peak this afternoon through the overnight \nhours. \n\nWith effective wind shear increasing to upwards of 50 knots \nacross the region this afternoon, isolated strong to severe \nthunderstorms are possible with damaging wind gusts and quick \nbursts of heavy rain being the primary threats. We have medium \nconfidence in this potential as we did see some clearing across\nthe region this morning, but with cloud cover already \nincreasing, we may not be able to tap into peak daytime heating \nto give storms that extra boost of energy. Regardless of storm \nstrength, 40-50 mph wind gusts are anticipated across much of \neastern Utah and western Colorado through the overnight hours. \nAs a result, a Wind Advisory is in effect through 6 AM Monday. \nIt won't take much enhancement from convective activity to see a\nfew damaging wind gusts over 58 mph. Stronger gusts are also \npossible over the higher terrain of eastern Utah. \n\nDue to fast storm motions, flooding impacts may be hard to come\nby unless we see stronger storms start to train over the same \nlocations. With soils becoming more saturated and an array of \nflood- prone terrain across the forecast area, we will continue\nto monitor the potential for flooding and flash flooding over \nthe next 24 hours, but confidence in flooding impacts is not \nhigh enough to issue a Flood Watch at this time. \n\nCONTINUED WET WEATHER THROUGH MID-WEEK: \n\nAfter the main surge of tropical moisture moves off to the \nnortheast early tomorrow morning, PWATs remain elevated enough \nfor continued showers and isolated storms on Monday and \nTuesday, especially over the higher terrain. Breezy winds \nremain in place as well with an upper-level in place ahead of a \nvery slow- moving low pressure system. Therefore, plenty of \nsynoptic support will be in place to bring precipitation to the \nvalleys from time to time. There's still some uncertainty in the\nensemble guidance, but generally speaking, low pressure breaks \ndown late Wednesday with northwesterly flow driving the forecast\nin its wake. This will bring cooler and drier conditions to the\nforecast area for the latter half of the week. \n\nA cold front is expected to drop across the region as the low \npressure system moves through. This will drop snow levels to \n8,000- 10,000 feet by Wednesday. As a result, area mountain \nranges will see their first widespread shot of snow for the \nseason. There is a 30- 60% chance of minor winter weather \nimpacts at pass level over the Uintas and San Juans, with lesser\nprobabilities elsewhere. Be ready for sloppy winter driving \nconditions, but broadly speaking we are not anticipating \nwidespread winter weather impacts at this time.",
